{"name":"Blue Jay Crest","hex":"#01708a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"jay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Yan-nhangu."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the numerous species of birds belonging to several genera within the family Corvidae, including Garrulus, Cyanocitta, Aphelocoma, Perisoreus, Cyanocorax, Gymnorhinus, Cyanolyca, Ptilostomus, and Calocitta, allied to the crows, but smaller, more graceful in form, often handsomely coloured, usually having a crest, and often noisy.","Any of various other birds of similar appearance and behaviour.\nThe Indian roller, Coracias benghalensis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jay"},{"term":"crest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The summit of a hill or mountain ridge.","A tuft, or other natural ornament, growing on an animal's head, for example the comb of a cockerel, the swelling on the head of a snake, the lengthened feathers of the crown or nape of bird, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Particularly with reference to waves, to reach a peak.","To reach the crest of (e.g. a hill or mountain)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crest"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue jay","blocks":[{"text":"The blue jay is a passerine bird in the family Corvidae, native to eastern North America. It lives in most of the eastern and central United States; some eastern populations may be migratory. Resident populations are also in Newfoundland, Canada; breeding populations are found across southern Canada. It breeds in both deciduous and coniferous forests, and is common in residential areas. Its coloration is predominantly blue, with a white chest and underparts, and a blue crest; it has a black, U-shaped collar around its neck and a black border behind the crest. Males and females are similar in size and plumage, which does not vary throughout the year. Four subspecies have been recognized."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Blue_jay_in_PP_%2830960%29.jpg/330px-Blue_jay_in_PP_%2830960%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_jay","wikidata":"Q199758"},{"title":"Jay","blocks":[{"text":"Jays are a paraphyletic grouping of passerine birds within the family Corvidae. Although the term \"jay\" is a folk taxonomy, most or all of the birds referred to as jays share a few similarities; they are small to medium-sized, usually have brightly coloured feathers and short tails, and are quite noisy. These superficial characteristics set them apart from most other corvids such as crows, ravens, jackdaws, rooks and magpies, which are mostly larger, or longer-tailed, and have darker plumage. Many so-called \"jays\" are genetically closer to these other corvids than to other jays, however. The name 'jay' is probably onomatopoeic, based on the raspy call of the species originally so named, Garrulus glandarius."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Eurasian_jay_%2800568%29.jpg/330px-Eurasian_jay_%2800568%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jay","wikidata":"Q10968813"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/102_Blue_Jay.jpg/500px-102_Blue_Jay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/102_Blue_Jay.jpg/1280px-102_Blue_Jay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/102_Blue_Jay.jpg/500px-102_Blue_Jay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John James Audubon drawing circa 1830s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A102_Blue_Jay.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Blue_jay_in_Central_Park_%2816465%29.jpg/500px-Blue_jay_in_Central_Park_%2816465%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Blue_jay_in_Central_Park_%2816465%29.jpg/1280px-Blue_jay_in_Central_Park_%2816465%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Blue_jay_in_Central_Park_%2816465%29.jpg/500px-Blue_jay_in_Central_Park_%2816465%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_jay_in_Central_Park_(16465).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Blue_Jay_Ash_RWD5.jpg/500px-Blue_Jay_Ash_RWD5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Blue_Jay_Ash_RWD5.jpg/1280px-Blue_Jay_Ash_RWD5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Blue_Jay_Ash_RWD5.jpg/500px-Blue_Jay_Ash_RWD5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_Jay_Ash_RWD5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Cyanocitta_cristata_cyanotephra%2C_Lubbock%2C_Texas_2.jpg/500px-Cyanocitta_cristata_cyanotephra%2C_Lubbock%2C_Texas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Cyanocitta_cristata_cyanotephra%2C_Lubbock%2C_Texas_2.jpg/1280px-Cyanocitta_cristata_cyanotephra%2C_Lubbock%2C_Texas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Cyanocitta_cristata_cyanotephra%2C_Lubbock%2C_Texas_2.jpg/500px-Cyanocitta_cristata_cyanotephra%2C_Lubbock%2C_Texas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACyanocitta_cristata_cyanotephra%2C_Lubbock%2C_Texas_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Cyanocitta_cristata_semplei%2C_Broward%2C_Florida_01.jpg/500px-Cyanocitta_cristata_semplei%2C_Broward%2C_Florida_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Cyanocitta_cristata_semplei%2C_Broward%2C_Florida_01.jpg/1280px-Cyanocitta_cristata_semplei%2C